{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cfw8aGxGHNd4jWDDEXi86","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## First-principles sizing\n\n### 1. Solar resource (the constraint that sets array size)\n\nDesign to the **worst month**, not the annual average — a system that balances in December works year-round; one sized to July averages will starve the battery every winter.\n\nAt 35°N in NM high desert (Albuquerque/Santa Fe-like climate), for a south-facing array at roughly latitude tilt:\n- Annual average: ~6.0–6.5 peak sun hours (PSH)/day\n- Worst month (December): ~**4.5–5.5 PSH**/day\n\nThis is a key feature of this climate — unlike cloudy regions, winter PSH doesn't collapse (short days + low sun angle are offset by near-zero cloud cover). Tilting steeper than latitude (lat+15°, ~50°) favors winter production and can push December PSH toward the higher end of that range.\n\nI'll design around **5.0 PSH** for the worst month as a reasonable, slightly conservative midpoint.\n\n### 2. System losses (derate factor)\n\nEnergy leaks between \"panel nameplate watts\" and \"watts the load actually uses\":\n\n| Loss source | Factor |\n|---|---|\n| Charge controller (MPPT) | ~0.97 |\n| Wiring/connections | ~0.98 |\n| Battery round-trip (LiFePO4 is efficient here) | ~0.95 |\n| Inverter (DC→AC) | ~0.94 |\n| Dust/soiling, temperature, mismatch | ~0.95 |\n\nCombined: **≈0.78–0.80 overall derate**. Use 0.78 to be safe.\n\n### 3. Panel array sizing\n\n$$\\text{Array (W)} = \\frac{\\text{Daily load (Wh)} / \\text{derate}}{\\text{worst-month PSH}} = \\frac{1500 / 0.78}{5.0} \\approx \\frac{1923}{5.0} \\approx 385\\text{ W}$$\n\n**Theoretical minimum: ~400 W.**\n\nBut don't stop there — panels are the cheapest part of an off-grid system per watt (~$0.30–0.50/W retail), while being undersized means brownouts every overcast week in January. Standard off-grid practice is to oversize the array 1.3–1.7× the bare minimum to cover:\n- A string of cloudy/snowy days where you still need *some* recharge\n- Panel degradation (~0.5%/yr)\n- Underestimated load (very likely — see note below)\n\n**Practical recommendation: 600–800 W array.**\n\n### 4. Battery bank sizing (3 days autonomy)\n\nAutonomy means: if the array produces *nothing* for 3 days, the battery alone must cover the load.\n\n$$\\text{Energy to deliver} = 3 \\times 1.5\\text{ kWh} = 4.5\\text{ kWh at the AC loads}$$\n\nAccount for inverter loss on the way out (already-stored energy, not generation, so only the inverter stage applies):\n$$4.5 / 0.94 \\approx 4.8\\text{ kWh drawn from the battery}$$\n\nLiFePO4 tolerates deep discharge (unlike lead-acid's 50% DoD), but for cycle-life longevity and a margin against cold-weather capacity fade, size for **~80% usable DoD**, not 100%:\n\n$$\\text{Nominal battery capacity} = 4.8 / 0.8 \\approx 6.0\\text{ kWh}$$\n\n**Recommendation: ~5.5–6 kWh nominal LiFePO4 bank.**\n\nOne NM-specific caveat: LiFePO4 charge *acceptance* drops sharply near/below freezing, and high-desert nights regularly go below freezing even when days are sunny. If the battery lives in an unheated space, either budget for a battery with built-in heating pads or keep it in a conditioned area — otherwise effective winter capacity (and chargeability) will be lower than this number assumes.\n\n## Bottom line\n\n| Component | Minimum | Recommended |\n|---|---|---|\n| Panel array | ~400 W | **600–800 W** |\n| Battery bank (LiFePO4, 3-day autonomy, 80% DoD) | ~5.5 kWh | **~6 kWh nominal** |\n\n## One flag worth raising\n\n1.5 kWh/day for two adults living full-time, year-round, is very low — roughly enough for LED lighting, phone/laptop charging, and a small efficient water pump, but it has no headroom for a refrigerator (typically 1–2 kWh/day alone), induction cooking, power tools, or winter heating-system electronics (igniters, blowers, circulators). If any of those are in the picture — even a propane fridge still needs some electronics — it's worth re-tallying the load list before locking in these numbers, since both the array and battery scale linearly with it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21838,"cache_read_input_tokens":19009,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21838},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7199c100-cf87-4624-99f9-1e9c24404829","uuid":"92a3e8e3-dcce-49e4-bbaa-de4751d48f41","timestamp":"2026-10-11T21:07:48.335Z","request_id":"req_011Cfw8aGisCMom4525SkapX"}
